Falsifies `paging::aarch64::a_table_entry_is_never_a_block`.

The defect: a table descriptor is encoded valid but without bit 1, which on VMSAv8-64 above L3
is the bit that says TABLE rather than BLOCK. `0b01` is a block descriptor, and the encoder
leaving the bit out looks like a tidy simplification because the other bit, `VALID`, is the one
whose name sounds load-bearing. The hardware then stops the walk at the intermediate entry and
maps a 1 GiB or 2 MiB block whose physical address is the address of a PAGE TABLE, so a process
gets the kernel's page tables mapped as ordinary memory. That is the whole isolation gone, and
it is silent: the mapping works.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!Aarch64::is_block(Aarch64::table_entry(pa)))`, for every `pa`,
because `is_block` on this format is exactly `entry & TABLE_OR_PAGE == 0`.

Note that the same bit at L3 means PAGE, which is why this one bit carries two names in the
source; the harness pins the meaning that matters above the leaf.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs b/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/aarch64.rs
@@ -133,7 +133,7 @@ impl PageFormat for Aarch64 {
         // A table descriptor: address plus valid/table bits, no attributes. Permissions on an
         // intermediate table would be an additional restriction on everything beneath it; we want
         // the leaf to be the single source of truth.
-        (pa & ADDR_MASK) | TABLE_OR_PAGE | VALID
+        (pa & ADDR_MASK) | VALID
     }
 
     fn leaf_entry(pa: u64, flags: Flags) -> u64 {
